Good — you finally narrowed the scope to a single artifact. Public test cases are provided, one file per feature, and the 'request' op maps to feature1_request. Here is the full test case JSON, verbatim:

Here are the public test cases for feature1_request:
{
  "description": "Issue an HTTP request through the client. The client opens a request with the requested method (defaulting to a GET when none is given), forwards every supplied request header to the underlying transport, and sends the request body when provided. The contract reports whether the request was actually dispatched, the resolved method, the target URL, each forwarded header, and the body.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "request",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo",
          "headers": {
            "a": "b"
          }
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "dispatched=true\nmethod=get\nurl=/foo\nheader[a]=b\nbody_present=false"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "request",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo",
          "method": "POST",
          "body": "hello",
          "headers": {
            "a": "b",
            "c": "d"
          }
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "dispatched=true\nmethod=POST\nurl=/foo\nheader[a]=b\nheader[c]=d\nbody_present=true\nbody=hello"
    }
  ]
}

Read it twice. Stdin is a single JSON object with op='request' and a nested 'request' object carrying url, optional method, optional headers, optional body. Stdout is exact key=value lines — dispatched, resolved method, url, one header[k]=v line per forwarded header in order, then body_present and body only when a body exists. Byte-for-byte. And note: this contract defines no error cases. If your adapter sprouts an error envelope, that's invention, not specification. Come back when you want the next feature's file — one at a time.

The 'read' op maps to feature 2, and feature 2 ships two contract files: text mode and json mode. You asked for the read feature, so here are both, verbatim.

Here are the public test cases for feature2_1_read_text:
{
  "description": "Read the response body as plain text. The text reader resolves to the raw response payload exactly as received from the transport.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "read",
        "mode": "text",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo"
        },
        "transport": {
          "responseText": "plain body"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "text=plain body"
    }
  ]
}

Here are the public test cases for feature2_2_read_json:
{
  "description": "Read the response body as parsed JSON. The JSON reader parses the response payload and exposes its fields. Keys are emitted in sorted order so the decoded object can be verified deterministically.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "read",
        "mode": "json",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo"
        },
        "transport": {
          "responseText": "{\"a\":\"b\"}"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "json[a]=b"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "read",
        "mode": "json",
        "request": {
          "url": "/u"
        },
        "transport": {
          "responseText": "{\"name\":\"ada\",\"age\":36}"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "json[age]=36\njson[name]=ada"
    }
  ]
}

Three observations, then you're on your own. One: the stdin shape is op='read' plus a 'mode' discriminator ('text' | 'json'), a nested 'request' object, and a 'transport' stub carrying responseText — the harness injects the payload, so your reader reads what the transport provides, nothing more. Two: json mode emits json[key]=value lines in sorted key order — see {'name':'ada','age':36} resolving to json[age] before json[name]. That sorting is part of the contract, not a suggestion. Three: neither file defines an error case. Don't invent one. Next feature when you're ready.

The 'response' op maps to feature3_response. One case file, verbatim:

Here are the public test cases for feature3_response:
{
  "description": "Inspect the response object. The response exposes the final (possibly redirected) URL and a clone operation that returns a distinct response object carrying the same observable data. The contract reports the response URL, whether the clone is a different object than the original, and the clone's URL.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "response",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo"
        },
        "transport": {
          "responseURL": "/foo?redirect"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "url=/foo?redirect\nclone_is_same_object=false\nclone.url=/foo?redirect"
    }
  ]
}

Three observations. One: the transport stub injects responseURL — your response wrapper surfaces it as the url property, and here it deliberately differs from the requested /foo to prove you're reporting the final URL, not the input. Two: the contract demands a real clone — clone_is_same_object=false means a distinct object, and clone.url must equal the original's final URL. A pass-through reference fails. Three: again, no error case in the contract. Don't fabricate one. Feature 4 when you're ready.

The 'headers' op maps to feature4_headers. One case file, verbatim:

Here are the public test cases for feature4_headers:
{
  "description": "Query response headers case-insensitively. Header lookups ignore case for the field name. When the same field appears multiple times its values are joined with commas in arrival order. A present field with an empty value is still reported as present with an empty string. Looking up an absent field yields an empty value and a not-present result.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "headers",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo"
        },
        "transport": {
          "responseHeaders": "X-Foo: bar\nX-Foo:baz"
        },
        "queries": [
          "x-foo",
          "x-missing"
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "get[x-foo]=bar,baz\nhas[x-foo]=true\nget[x-missing]=\nhas[x-missing]=false"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "headers",
        "request": {
          "url": "/foo"
        },
        "transport": {
          "responseHeaders": "Server: \nX-Foo:baz"
        },
        "queries": [
          "server",
          "X-foo"
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "get[server]=\nhas[server]=true\nget[X-foo]=baz\nhas[X-foo]=true"
    }
  ]
}

Three observations, and these are landmines. One: stdin carries a raw 'responseHeaders' string in wire format — name:value pairs separated by newlines, with sloppy spacing (colon followed by a space in one entry, no space in the next). Your header parser normalizes that, not the harness. Two: duplicates join with commas in arrival order — 'bar,baz' — and lookups are case-insensitive in both directions: query 'x-foo' matches header 'X-Foo', query 'X-foo' matches too. Three: the empty-value trap — 'Server: ' with nothing after the colon must yield get[server]= (empty string) but has[server]=true. An absent field is the mirror image: empty get, false has. Missing any one of these edge cases fails the contract. Feature 5 when you're ready.

The 'delegate' op maps to feature5_native_delegation. One case file, verbatim:

Here are the public test cases for feature5_native_delegation:
{
  "description": "Delegate to the native client when one is available. When the runtime already provides a native HTTP client, the exported function delegates calls to it rather than using the fallback transport. The delegating function works even when invoked without a receiver object, and forwards the requested URL unchanged to the native client.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "delegate",
        "url": "/native"
      },
      "expected_output": "delegated=native\nnative_received_url=/native\nresult=native:/native"
    }
  ]
}

Three observations. One: the stdin shape here is flat — op='delegate' with a bare top-level 'url', no nested 'request' object. Different ops, different payloads; your adapter handles each contract as given. Two: the subtlety this feature exists for — the exported function must remain callable detached, without a receiver object, i.e. via a bound or arrow-wrapped reference to the native client. Call it bare, as the harness does, and it must still delegate. A method that relies on 'this' breaks here. Three: the URL is forwarded to the native client unchanged — '/native' in, '/native' out — and the native client's return value passes through untouched. No rewriting, no wrapping. Note there is no explicit has_native_fetch flag in this op; the harness simulates the native environment for you. Feature 6 when you're ready — and it comes in three files, so pace yourself.

The 'resolve' op maps to feature 6, and as warned it ships as three case files: browser entry, universal-in-browser, and universal-in-server. All three, verbatim.

Here are the public test cases for feature6_1_resolver_browser_entry:
{
  "description": "Browser-targeted resolver. In a browser-like environment the resolver selects the runtime's native client when one exists, otherwise it falls back to the bundled polyfill transport. The resolved transport routes the requested URL unchanged. The contract reports which transport was selected and the URL it routed.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "resolve",
        "entry": "browser",
        "has_native_fetch": true,
        "url": "/p"
      },
      "expected_output": "selected=native\nrouted_url=/p"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "resolve",
        "entry": "browser",
        "has_native_fetch": false,
        "url": "/p"
      },
      "expected_output": "selected=polyfill\nrouted_url=/p"
    }
  ]
}

Here are the public test cases for feature6_2_resolver_universal_browser:
{
  "description": "Universal entry resolver in a browser-like environment. Using the universal entry point inside a browser-like runtime, the resolver selects the native client when one exists, otherwise it falls back to the bundled polyfill transport. The resolved transport routes the requested URL unchanged.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "resolve",
        "entry": "main",
        "server": false,
        "has_native_fetch": true,
        "url": "/p"
      },
      "expected_output": "selected=native\nrouted_url=/p"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "resolve",
        "entry": "main",
        "server": false,
        "has_native_fetch": false,
        "url": "/p"
      },
      "expected_output": "selected=polyfill\nrouted_url=/p"
    }
  ]
}

Here are the public test cases for feature6_3_resolver_universal_server:
{
  "description": "Universal entry resolver in a server-like environment. Using the universal entry point inside a server-like runtime, the resolver selects the native client when one exists. When no native client exists it routes through the server-side HTTP transport, which additionally normalizes a protocol-relative URL (leading double slash) into an absolute secure URL before issuing the request.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "resolve",
        "entry": "main",
        "server": true,
        "has_native_fetch": true,
        "url": "/p"
      },
      "expected_output": "selected=native\nrouted_url=/p"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "resolve",
        "entry": "main",
        "server": true,
        "has_native_fetch": false,
        "url": "//p"
      },
      "expected_output": "selected=server\nrouted_url=https://p"
    }
  ]
}

Three observations, and the third is the one teams flunk. One: the stdin shape is flat — 'entry' ('browser' | 'main'), a 'server' boolean for the universal entry, 'has_native_fetch', and a 'url'. Precedence is fixed: native always wins when present, regardless of entry or environment; browser entry never selects the server transport — only the universal entry in a server-like environment can. Two: '/p' routes unchanged everywhere. Three: the normalization lives ONLY in the server transport — '//' + 'p' becomes 'https://p' on that path alone. If you apply that rewrite in the polyfill or native path, you've leaked policy into the wrong layer and you fail. That's all six features released. The observable contract is now fully on the table — build to it exactly, and don't invent beyond it.
